# Databases — CLAUDE.md > Data safety: real DBs exist only on the server.
# Nothing matching these may ever be committed.
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# SQLite sidecars hold the same rows as the database itself.
*-wal
*-shm
*-journal

# Exception: fake-real.db is a deliberate decoy holding no data. It is tracked
# on purpose so the guard hook always has a fixture to be tested against.
# See CLAUDE.md > Data safety.
!fake-real.db

# Secrets — Plaid tokens, keys.
.env
.env.*

# A friend's raw transcript, pulled beside spec.md by scripts/pull-spec.sh so
# the builder can read what they meant. NEVER committed: spec.md is a designed
# artifact describing a dashboard, and this is everything they said, including
# whatever they said around it. The record of record is the append-only
# `transcripts` table on the droplet. The guard hook denies .db and .env, not
# markdown — these two lines and tests/repo/gitignore.test.ts are the whole
# defence. See CLAUDE.md > Data safety.
#
# TWO LINES, because the transcript exists at more than one path. The second
# covers the sidecars scripts/write-spec-pair.ts writes next to it —
# `.conversation.md.tmp`, which holds the transcript byte-for-byte during every
# single pull, and `.conversation.md.bak`, which that file's rollback
# DELIBERATELY leaves behind when a restore fails (a named residual: at that
# moment the .bak is the only surviving copy). Git does not ignore dotfiles, so
# without the second line `git add -A` stages a whole transcript at a perfectly
# ordinary path. Keep the two in step with the `.${name}.tmp` / `.${name}.bak`
# names in scripts/write-spec-pair.ts, which points back at this block.
#
# Deliberately NOT `users/*/*conversation.md*` or anything wider: spec.md is
# tracked, and users/<slug>/notes/conversation.md is a note somebody wrote by
# hand and may well want committed. `*` does not cross a `/`, which is what
# keeps the second one out of these patterns.
users/*/conversation.md
users/*/.conversation.md.*

# Python bytecode. Nothing here generated any until the shared Plaid module
# arrived: every seed.py before it was self-contained, so importing
# modules/plaid/seed_plaid.py from users/<slug>/seed.py is what first created a
# __pycache__ — and it is machine-specific, version-stamped
# (cpython-311.pyc) and worthless as history.
#
# It was caught by the pre-commit test gate rather than by anything that knows
# what a .pyc is: the gate saw a change under modules/ with no matching test
# and blocked. Correct outcome, unrelated reason.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Scratch: git worktrees live under .claude/, which is otherwise tracked.
.claude/worktrees/

# Next.js build artifacts
.next/
out/
# Anchored to repo root: an unanchored `build/` also matches lib/build/ and
# tests/build/ (this repo's convention for build-notes parsing code), which
# silently blocked `git add` on real source. Root output is the only thing
# this line was ever meant to ignore.
/build/
next-env.d.ts
.vercel

# TypeScript incremental build cache
*.tsbuildinfo

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Screenshot review artifacts (onboarding ledger D16). Kept on disk so a
# review has evidence rather than a claim, never committed — they are large,
# they change on every styling edit, and they are worthless as history.
.screenshots/
test-results/

# Local credentials scratch file. The pattern here had a trailing 's' and so
# matched nothing, leaving logins.txt tracked-able in the repo root.
logins.txt
